sec.p:
Custom-made Matlab scripts were used to analyze the open field behaviour. The arena was seen as a coordinate system with an x- and y-axis, and the coordinates of the center of the arena were defined as:


center(X)=min(X)+(max(X)−min(X)2),center(Y)=min(Y)+(max(Y)−min(Y)2)
\begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} & \operatorname{center}(X)=\min (X)+\left(\frac{\max (X)-\min (X)}{2}\right), \\ & \operatorname{center}(Y)=\min (Y)+\left(\frac{\max (Y)-\min (Y)}{2}\right)\end{aligned}$$\end{document}

With the instantaneous coordinates of the fly (Xi, Yi) and the center coordinates, wall-following (WAFO) of the fly was calculated in pixels by applying the Pythagorean theorem:


WAFO=(xi−center(X))2+(yi−center(Y))2
\begin{document}$$\displaystyle \mathrm{WAFO}=\sqrt{\left(x_{i}-\operatorname{center}(X)\right)^{2}+\left(y_{i}-\operatorname{center}(Y)\right)^{2}}$$\end{document}

With the instantaneous coordinates of the fly (Xi, Yi) and the coordinates on the next frame (instant location i+1), total walking activity (TOWA) of the fly during the 10 min recording was calculated in pixels by applying the summation:


TOWA=∑i(xi−xi+1)2+(yi−yi+1)2
\begin{document}$$\displaystyle =\sum i \sqrt{\left(x_{i}-x_{i+1}\right)^{2}+\left(y_{i}-y_{i+1}\right)^{2}}$$\end{document}
